Z||000000||four50four||11-06-1999||08:15 PM||||well, though i don't have a fiero yet   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, when i do get one, I am seriously considering getting turbo as well   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (strokers??) i got some questions though:<P>1. is it necessary to do a engine rebuild if the engine is high miles? i would like to find a fiero with a blown engine already, so i can start with a 3.2<BR>2. how hard will it be to install, compared to removal and installation of a minivan engine?<BR>3. what kind of gas milage can i expect, say, compared to a 350 chev?<BR>4. with the adjustable boost, can i run it at low boost (1-4 lbs) to save gas, then turn it up without having to get out?<BR>5. what kind of performace will i get, say, against 5.0 stangs, assuming i have a 5-speed<BR>6. any idea how it will sound?<BR>7. will i need a stronger clutch?<BR>8. how will this effect engine life?<P>i know these are a lot of questions, but any response to any of them would be greatly appreciated. thanks so much!<p>[This message has been edited by four50four (edited 11-06-1999).]||||reg||2
Z||000001||fierospeeder||11-07-1999||04:25 AM||||i own a 87 tbird turbo coupe so im using my experience from that.  I have a fiero also but havn't done the upgrade.  You need to have forged pistons because if your running lean you will melt the pistons and you also have to run higher octane.  You can adjust boost by running a vaccum line into the cabin and having a valve you adjust manually like a fish tank air valve.  Or you can buy an expensive electronic one to control.  Its suggested that a detenator sensor be added so when its running lean the engine will decrease the timing curve to prevent detenation.  My tbird came stock with an intercooler, it cools the air making allowing more boost.  Its not really rough on the engine unless you are going to be turning up on the boost at very high levels which you might be blowing gaskets or hurting bearnings.  You can also destroy your turbo if you overboost and the turbo cant take it.  Your fuel economy will be better.  The turbo only kicks in when you floor it.  Just crusing around town, there is no boost, so your just running as a normal engine.  When you start pressing down on the pedal, at a certain rpm which is high, the pressure in the exhause will build spinning the turbo, creating boost and it will feel like you got a bigger engine.  Thats all i can tell you about a turbo.  It also creates a lot of heat because it uses the exhaust for power.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||turbo86se||11-07-1999||12:34 PM||||Four50four, I've had a MillerWoods turbo on my 86SE for eight years now, with no problems at all. The turbo unit is very reliable, efficient, and certainly gets the adrenaline pumping! My Fiero is going to turn 51,000 miles very soon, and the turbo was installed around 20,000. It's an automatic,too. As for your questions, gas mileage is great, except for when you run the turbo, that eats it up! I always use 93 octane gas. The exhaust is modified with ported and polished manifolds, also ceramic coated by Jet Hot.Factory muffler, removed cat. convertor (too much heat!). If you listen hard, or have the windows down, you can hear the hiss of the turbo at boost, and it's awesome. The only giveaway to my system are the custom TURBO sail panels from Paul Kribben. I don't even waste my gas on Mustangs, they can't keep up. Last fall I beat an early 90's Vette, and he had a head start on me. If you're anywhere near Myrtle Beach, you're welcome for a test drive! I don't drive the car much, it's in immaculate condition, and I guess I am a little "afraid" of the car!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">)     <BR>Off to the Senior Tour!!||||reg||10
Z||000003||turbo86se||11-07-1999||12:37 PM||||Oh yeah, the turbo is putting out between 8-10 psi at boost!||||reg||1
