Z||000007||Eric||11-07-1999||07:06 PM||||Sometimes you don't get all your questions answered by a "search".  454, were you planning on doing a rebuild or an engine swap?  I hope you are planning on a rebuild it is a little more costly and time consuming, but you can get a lot more perfomance than just a standard minivan swap.  You can build a 3.1 with all the trick parts like forged pistons, roller valvetrain and a crazy clutch, in hopes of installing a turbo one day.  But you must recall that a naturally aspirated 3.1 can make over 200 hp and 220ft/lbs of torque.  That is sufficiant ammo to shoot down a few Mustangs in such a light car.  The money you would save by just deciding to stay 3.1 N.A. could buy you another Fiero.  That's my logic anyway.  I'm building a regular 3.1.  Should be cool!||||reg||1
Z||000008||turbo86se||11-08-1999||12:21 AM||||I agree with you, Eric, rebuilding a 2.8 into a 3.1 or 3.2 is much more cost-effective than purchasing and installing a turbo unit. Before I moved to SC, I lived in Frederick, MD. Only 25 minutes from IRM. They installed the turbo on my car. I believe the turbo cost almost $3000, and a lot more to install. Maybe next winter I'll install a 3.4 block from a Camaro and use the turbo for some serious power... back to reality! 88Fiero, I know Dennis LaGura has made his own turbo set up in his GT, and from what I hear, it's very nice and efficient. If he doesn't already, maybe he'll start selling it in kit form. My turbo is made by MillerWoods, they're out of buisness now, but the same man, Kevin Leslie (I think) now runs Design One, a more improved turbo system than the one I have. I hear costs are the same.  ||||reg||1
